{#- _partials/_cast_null_check.py.j2 — the type-cast failure count
    (p64 R9.1/R9.3, design C10, task 2e.1).

    THE DEFECT THIS EXISTS FOR. Spark's `cast` returns NULL on failure rather
    than raising. A source value of `"twenty-five"` cast to `int` becomes NULL,
    and from that point on it is indistinguishable from a row whose source
    genuinely had no value. The DQ `Completeness` rule counts both the same way,
    which is how a bad value passes as missing data. R9.1 records that this — not
    a type rule — is what actually caught `"twenty-five"` in the reliability
    report's Test 7; no type rule exists anywhere in the generated job. R9.3
    requires the two be distinguishable in whatever is reported.

    THE MOMENT OF THE CONVERSION IS THE ONLY PLACE THE ANSWER IS CHEAP, which is
    C10's mechanism and the reason this block sits BEFORE the auto-cast loop
    rather than after it. Both the pre-conversion value and the target type are
    in hand here. One statement later the column has been rebound to the
    converted value, and `col(name).isNotNull()` is then False for exactly the
    cells being looked for — so a predicate accumulated now and evaluated after
    the loop would report zero on every run. This is not a style preference: it is
    the difference between a working count and one that is silently always zero.

    IT COUNTS CELLS, NOT ROWS, AND IT LOSES NEITHER. Every row continues to the
    target with the NULL in place. The number is how many (row, column) values
    were nulled BY the conversion, which is a different quantity from a row count
    and is deliberately not comparable with one.

    WHY THE FINDING GOES ON THE `checks[]` LIST AND NOT THE
    `(run_id, "load_metrics")` ROW. Task 2c.4 put `rows_deduplicated` on that row
    because R7.6's decomposition is an arithmetic identity over ROWS —
    rows_read = rows_written + rows_dropped + rows_quarantined — and a dedup drop
    is a term in that sum. A conversion NULL is not a term in it: the row is
    written, in full, with one field nulled, so adding a cell count to that row
    would let a reader summing its terms over-count losses that never happened.
    Two further reasons, both about where the wrong conclusion is formed:

      * The place a SOURCE null is reported is the `Completeness` rule's outcome
        in the data quality report's `checks[]`. R9.3's "distinguishable" is only
        satisfied if the other half of the distinction is legible in the same
        document; on a separate row it would be a true number nobody reads
        beside the claim it corrects.
      * It is a finding that is not a rule outcome, which is a shape task 2d.3
        already settled. Its three constraints are inherited verbatim below.

    2d.3's CONSTRAINTS, INHERITED RATHER THAN RE-DECIDED. Status is `warning`,
    the third value `QualityCheck.status` admits — there is no fourth, and
    `fail` is reserved for an outcome that removed rows from the frame, which
    this did not. The entry is counted in NONE of `rules_passed` /
    `rules_failed` / `rules_total`, which describe the DQDL ruleset. It moves
    neither `overall_status` nor `rows_quarantined`.

    WHERE THE ENTRY IS EMITTED: inline in `_partials/_data_quality.py.j2`'s
    'report' stage, under a duplicate of this file's guard, NOT from here. The
    report row is one `put_item` on a PutItem-only role (`LGR-9e76249-3`), so a
    second writer clobbers rather than merges, and the entry has to join the
    `_dq_checks` list already being built inside that single write.

    THE APPEND IS ADDITIONALLY GUARDED ON THE LOCAL EXISTING, and that guard is
    not decoration — it is what makes this partial's narrower reach safe. The DQ
    partial renders on all five templates; this one can only render where an
    auto-cast block exists. So a Glue-Catalog- or Parquet-target pipeline that
    sets the key would reach an append whose local was never defined, and because
    the whole report write sits inside `except Exception as _ddb_err`, the
    consequence would not be a crash but the SILENT LOSS OF THE ENTIRE DQ REPORT
    ROW. That is a worse failure than the one this feature removes.
    `_data_quality.py.j2` therefore tests membership in `locals()` before
    building the entry, so an unmeasured run simply has no entry.

    ABSENT IS NOT ZERO, which is why that append is skipped rather than defaulted.
    "No conversion nulled a value" is a claim only a run that measured can make.
    Emitting `0` for a template that casts inside a `try` it never inspected would
    be the same class of lie R9.3 exists to remove, one level up. Task 2c.4 drew
    the same line for an omitted key versus a written zero.

    REACH, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. Two include sites, both S3 Tables arms:
    `glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2` and `glue_job_jdbc_source.py.j2`'s
    `target_type == 'S3_TABLES'` branch. Both use the same cached `_target_schema`
    local, which is what makes one partial serve both. NOT included at:

      * the two Glue Catalog arms (`glue_job_glue_catalog.py.j2`, and
        `glue_job_jdbc_source.py.j2`'s `GLUE_CATALOG` branch). Their cast loop
        runs inside a `try` whose schema comes from a runtime
        `create_dynamic_frame.from_catalog` read, so there is no equivalent local
        to read before the loop and the whole block is skipped when the target
        table does not exist yet. Wiring it there means restructuring that `try`,
        which changes rendered bytes for every existing Glue Catalog pipeline.
        DEFERRED AND CURRENTLY UNOWNED, recorded as design O8 rather than silently
        skipped. No task owns it: task 2e.2's text is R9.2, and neither of R9.2's
        clauses is "wire the count into the Glue Catalog arm" — earlier revisions of
        this header named 2e.2 as the owner, which was wrong. O8 also carries the
        reason deferring is not laziness: anything placed inside that `try` has its
        failures swallowed by `except Exception as _cast_err` and reported as
        "Skipping auto-cast (target table may not exist yet)", which is a misleading
        message about a silently broken count — the same class of lie R9 exists to
        remove, one level up.
      * `glue_job_jdbc.py.j2` and `glue_job_s3_parquet.py.j2`, which contain no
        conversion at all — so no conversion can null a value and there is
        nothing to count. That is an absence of the phenomenon rather than a
        coverage gap.

    ONE EXTRA SPARK ACTION, PAID ONLY INSIDE THE ENABLED ARM. All per-column sums
    go into a SINGLE `agg`, collected once — so the cost is one pass over the
    frame regardless of how many columns are being converted, not one per column.
    A pipeline that has not opted in pays nothing. That is the same bargain
    `_partials/_dedup.py.j2`'s two counts and `_nonprintable_check.py.j2`'s one
    strike.

    THE AGGREGATE IS ALIASED BY POSITION, NOT BY COLUMN NAME, and the row is read
    positionally to match. Aliasing by the schema's own field name would put a
    runtime-supplied string into an `alias(...)`, and while every target column
    name downstream has already passed p55 R11.3's rename projection, an index
    cannot be made to mean anything else at all. The parallel name list keeps the
    reporting readable without giving the alias a job it does not need.

    ZERO RENDERED BYTES WHEN DISABLED, exactly as `_partials/_dedup.py.j2` and
    `_partials/_nonprintable_check.py.j2` are. The whitespace arithmetic is the
    same one: the caller's `{%- include %}` strips the newline that ended the line
    above it, the outer `{%- if %}` and the blank line opening its body restore it
    when enabled, and `{%- endif -%}` strips the trailing one. Note the two
    include sites need DIFFERENT surrounding blank lines because the lines they
    sit between differ — `glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2` has a blank line at that point
    and `glue_job_jdbc_source.py.j2` does not. Do not tidy either side's minus
    signs independently, and do not assume the two sites are copy-identical;
    `test_cast_null_check_all_targets.py`'s byte-identity assertion is the fence.

    THE GUARD IS `is mapping and … is true`, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY **NOT** THE
    `is defined and` IDIOM `data_quality_enabled` AND `dqdl_rules` USE. Both
    sibling partials carry the full account; the short version is that `is defined`
    was tried and FAILED, because
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s
    `test_dq_block_survives_a_second_jinja2_copy_in_sys_modules` renders all five
    templates with no `quality_config` and two jinja2 copies loaded — under which
    `Context.resolve_or_missing` hands back one copy's `missing` sentinel while the
    compiled template tests identity against the other's. `_MissingType` is not an
    `Undefined`, so `is defined` answers True and the read behind it raises.
    `is mapping` is an `isinstance` check against `abc.Mapping`, which consults no
    sentinel, so its answer does not depend on how many jinja2 copies are loaded.
    It also fails CLOSED in every case: unset, `None`, a scalar, or a dict without
    the key all leave the count off.

    `.get(...)` for the same reason, and it is NOT the default-value filter
    do-NOT 6 forbids: that filter decides by asking whether its input is an
    `Undefined` instance, which is the identity question two jinja2 copies make
    unanswerable, whereas `.get` is a plain method on a value `is mapping` has
    already established is a real dict. That filter's name is deliberately not
    spelled anywhere in this file — the fence over it is a substring count across
    the whole file, so a header naming it would be counted as a violation of
    itself (`LGR-b46a5bb-4`). Both sibling partials carry the same note for the
    same reason.

    `is true` rather than plain truthiness mirrors `resolve_quality_config`'s own
    rule that only an explicit `bool` opts in, so the string `"false"` cannot mean
    enabled here either.

    NO PIPELINE-SUPPLIED IDENTIFIER REACHES THE EMITTED CODE. The column list is
    derived at runtime from `_target_schema` and `df_transformed.columns`, exactly
    as the auto-cast loop it shadows already does, so this partial opens NO
    generated-code sink: there is no name here for `validate_identifier` to guard
    and R8.5 / do-NOT 7's ordering rule is untouched by it. `LGR-819980f-1`
    (INVARIANT) is likewise unaffected — the only render variable interpolated is
    `quality_config`, and nothing is added to the `getResolvedOptions` key list.

    NO STAGE VARIABLE, for the reason `_dedup.py.j2`'s header gives: this partial
    has one emission point, so a stage argument would be ceremony with a second
    failure mode. That also keeps its whole context surface to the single variable
    `quality_config`, so `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s `P64_PARTIALS`
    fence covers it with no new exclusions.

    REQUIRED CONTEXT VARIABLE: `quality_config`, assigned unconditionally in
    `code_assembly.generate_code`'s `template_vars` from
    `resolve_quality_config`, which returns every recognised key on every call
    (R6.5 / do-NOT 6, design C7).

    REQUIRED CALLER LOCALS: `_target_schema` and `df_transformed`, both of which
    the auto-cast block immediately below every include site already reads.

    ALL of the rationale lives in this Jinja comment and none of it in the
    emitted Python, per `LGR-b46a5bb-4` (template / ratified): this repo's
    template fences are substring assertions over the RENDERED output, so naming
    a fenced token in emitted Python would be counted as an occurrence of it.
-#}
{%- if quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("cast_null_check_enabled") is true %}

    # === Type-cast failure count (p64 R9.1/R9.3) ===
    # Taken BEFORE the conversion below, because that is the only point at which
    # both the source value and the target type are in hand. A cell counted here
    # held a real source value that the conversion could not represent — it is
    # NOT a source that was empty, and the completeness rule cannot tell the two
    # apart. Counts cells, not rows: every row continues to the target.
    # One aggregate, one pass over the frame, however many columns convert.
    _adi_cast_null_cells = 0
    _adi_cast_null_detail = ""
    _adi_cast_names = []
    if _target_schema:
        from pyspark.sql import functions as _adi_cast_functions
        from pyspark.sql.types import DateType as _adi_cast_date_type
        from pyspark.sql.types import TimestampType as _adi_cast_timestamp_type

        _adi_cast_terms = []
        for _adi_cast_field in _target_schema:
            if _adi_cast_field.name not in df_transformed.columns:
                continue
            if df_transformed.schema[_adi_cast_field.name].dataType == _adi_cast_field.dataType:
                continue
            # Mirrors the three arms of the conversion below exactly. to_date and
            # to_timestamp null an unparseable value the same way cast does.
            _adi_cast_source = _adi_cast_functions.col(_adi_cast_field.name)
            if isinstance(_adi_cast_field.dataType, _adi_cast_date_type):
                _adi_cast_result = _adi_cast_functions.to_date(_adi_cast_source)
            elif isinstance(_adi_cast_field.dataType, _adi_cast_timestamp_type):
                _adi_cast_result = _adi_cast_functions.to_timestamp(_adi_cast_source)
            else:
                _adi_cast_result = _adi_cast_source.cast(_adi_cast_field.dataType)
            # Aliased by position; the parallel list carries the names.
            _adi_cast_terms.append(
                _adi_cast_functions.sum(
                    _adi_cast_functions.when(
                        _adi_cast_source.isNotNull() & _adi_cast_result.isNull(), 1
                    ).otherwise(0)
                ).alias(f"c{len(_adi_cast_terms)}")
            )
            _adi_cast_names.append(_adi_cast_field.name)
        if _adi_cast_terms:
            _adi_cast_row = df_transformed.agg(*_adi_cast_terms).collect()[0]
            _adi_cast_hits = {
                _adi_cast_names[_adi_cast_index]: int(_adi_cast_row[_adi_cast_index])
                for _adi_cast_index in range(len(_adi_cast_names))
                if _adi_cast_row[_adi_cast_index]
            }
            _adi_cast_null_cells = sum(_adi_cast_hits.values())
            _adi_cast_null_detail = ", ".join(
                f"{_adi_cast_column}={_adi_cast_count}"
                for _adi_cast_column, _adi_cast_count in sorted(_adi_cast_hits.items())
            )
    if _adi_cast_null_cells > 0:
        logger.warning(
            f"Type-cast failure count: {_adi_cast_null_cells} value(s) became NULL because the "
            f"source held something the target type cannot represent ({_adi_cast_null_detail}). "
            f"These had a source value and are not empty sources; the completeness rule cannot "
            f"tell them apart. Rows were kept."
        )
    else:
        logger.info(
            f"Type-cast failure count: every non-null source value converted cleanly across "
            f"{len(_adi_cast_names)} converting column(s)"
        )
    # === End Type-cast failure count ===
{%- endif -%}
